from the air caca van der Rohe in the  northern part of the Central African  Republic looks pretty peaceful but what  we're seeing is actually a refugee camp  housing around 30,000 people some of  them from the city itself half of those  in the camper children many have been  badly traumatized like Lucie blue shoe  she's 12  from a village about 90 kilometres away  Lucie was forced to flee when her home  was attacked by rebels  I have no family I live with other  people from my village a teacher from  the village tells us that her mother was  taken by the rebels Lucy hasn't heard  from her since everyone else and her  family is dead  stories like Lucy's are not uncommon one  morning they came to our village  they had really big guns and started  shooting at everything in sight they  even killed children I ran away with my  family and came to this camp after  school we accompanied our Kia back to  his new home in the refugee camp only  his grandfather is there to greet him  it's very hard to survive here there's  not much aid and we no longer have  fields to plant things to eat only God  can help us the camp went up a year ago  practically overnight after an armed  group attacked the Christian district of  the city seeking protection the people  there fled to UN troops stationed nearby  kaga bond arrow is a divided city the  small river that flows through its  geographic center serves as the border  the center in the northern districts is  in the hands of the Muslim rebel militia  called silicon the southern districts  are occupied by their largely Christian  opponents known as the anti-balaka many  houses are deserted here next to that  are the UN barracks the kaga Bandera  refugee camp grew up next to its  protective fence the blue helmets took  up positions on the city's only bridge  in an attempt to keep the two warring  sides apart but the raids continue there  are around 12,500 UN soldiers in the  country only a few residents have dared  to return to their homes Florentine Don  Betty lives in a hut built amidst the  wreckage of his former farm which was  destroyed by the Celica can't sleep here  anymore  a woman was robbed here yesterday  someone robbed her of 25 thousand francs  I spoke with the authorities about the  security situation here it's not safe in  this part of the city security donek de  Loup has worked for UNICEF in the  Central African Republic for years the  Kaka bandura camp is just one of many in  the country she says and things are  getting worse the humanitarian needs are  increasing sadly more and more people  are under attack our IDPs where now we  have 600,000 people who are internally  displaced within the country which is  what it was at the peak of the crisis in  2014 so there's still a lot to do among  the refugees tens of thousands of  children who were taken up by armed  groups the UN is trying to liberate as  many as possible from the Warlord's and  rebels but many struggle to reintegrate  into society many of the girls were kept  for years as sex slaves I was 12 and  lived in Bunge an armed group forced me  to go with them  since then I've lived around here I'm 17  now it was terrible  don't be like that I saw and did the  most awful things the life I had all  those years I was no life at all as I  say I used some villainy ain't gone  there's no safe haven for children in  this environment of violence at least  there's a playground in the camp but the  legacy of war is unmistakable even here  sometimes Akhil spends time with other  children in the camp in the afternoon  but nothing is like it used to be I had  a good life in my village I had lots of  friends we had a lot of fun together we  had school lessons under the big tree in  the evenings we sang and danced in the  moonlight but that's all gone many of my  friends are dead and the others are in  refugee camps  Lucia Nikhil share a similar fate and  they now face a highly uncertain future  and as it stands now a peaceful solution  between the warring parties seems  unlikely  you
 

